From 3ce2d615541860a5916ef90b24388d64a57aecff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 09:07:48 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 35/37] libkrun: restore krun_set_exec() for non-nitro builds

Driving a real containerd through the nerdbox shim on Windows 11 gets as far as
CreateTask with the right erofs/ext4/overlay mounts and then dies 48 ms into VM
setup:

    ctr: failed to create shim task: failed to set exec:
         krun_set_exec failed: -129

-129 is -ENOTSUP; the MSVC CRT numbers ENOTSUP 129 where glibc numbers it 95.
Nothing about that is a Windows fault. It is a version-pin mismatch, and it
fails identically on Linux and macOS.

Upstream 4d2201e ("libkrun: remove exec/env/workdir/rlimits support from
non-nitro builds", 2026-06-30) moved exec_path/args/env/workdir/rlimits out of
ContextConfig into a NitroConfig gated on cfg(feature = "aws-nitro"), left
-ENOTSUP stubs behind so libkrun.so.2 keeps exporting the symbols, and dropped
the corresponding injection from krun_start_enter(): the kernel command line
then carried only DEFAULT_KERNEL_CMDLINE, the block root, and whatever
krun_append_kernel_cmdline() had added. The replacement is Config::apply()
reading /.krun_config.json out of the guest rootfs. 4d2201e is an ancestor of
our pin 07fd40d (it is its parent), so we inherit the stubs.

nerdbox v0.2.3 builds libkrun v1.19.0 (Dockerfile, ARG LIBKRUN_VERSION=v1.19.0,
tagged 2026-06-09), three weeks before that commit, and its host code is written
to that API. internal/vm/libkrun/instance.go calls SetExec("/sbin/vminitd",
InitArgs, env) once, immediately after SetKernel and before console setup, vsock
configuration and krun_start_enter, and treats any non-zero return as fatal.
The gate carries no target_os condition, so every platform hits it.

Pinning libkrun back to v1.19.0 is not available to us: the WHP backend this
whole port stands on postdates that tag. Adopting the replacement would mean
teaching nerdbox to write /.krun_config.json into its guest image and dropping
the SetExec call -- a change to both the host code and the image build of a
second component we already carry patches for, to arrive at the same behaviour.
So the smaller divergence is here: restore what v1.19.0 did, in the one place
that has a caller.

Restoring the symbol alone would have been worse than the stub. A krun_set_exec
that stores a workload nobody reads returns zero, nerdbox proceeds through
console setup and vsock configuration into krun_start_enter, and the guest boots
with vminitd as PID 1 holding no argv and no environ -- no -vsock-rpc-port, no
-vsock-stream-port, no -vsock-cid -- so it never dials back and the failure
surfaces much later as a timeout with nothing pointing at its cause. The
plumbing therefore had to be checked before the setter was worth restoring, and
it survives:

  - KernelCmdlineConfig still has krun_env and epilog, and vmm's builder still
    splices both into the guest cmdline (src/vmm/src/builder.rs). krun_env goes
    in unconditionally, including when the caller supplied its own cmdline
    through krun_set_kernel() -- which is nerdbox's path, since it boots an
    external kernel with "root=/dev/vda rootfstype=erofs ro init=/sbin/vminitd".
    epilog is appended last, after the TSI and DHCP tokens, so the "--" stays at
    the end where the kernel expects it.
  - The guest side is untouched by 4d2201e. The kernel hands init every
    unrecognised KEY=value token as an environment variable and everything after
    "--" as init's argv[1..], which is all nerdbox's vminitd needs. libkrun's own
    init still reads KRUN_INIT and execs it, and still honours KRUN_WORKDIR and
    KRUN_RLIMITS (init/init-binary/src/main.rs, src/env.rs), so the restored
    tokens land where they always did.

Only krun_set_exec is restored. nerdbox binds nineteen symbols and calls
krun_set_env, krun_set_workdir and krun_set_rlimits from none of them -- the
env it cares about is the third argument to SetExec -- so those three stay
-ENOTSUP stubs rather than becoming API we diverge on and never exercise.

The injection is emitted only when krun_set_exec() was actually called. A caller
on upstream's .krun_config.json path leaves exec_path as None and gets the
byte-identical cmdline it got before this patch, rather than a stray trailing
"--" that v1.19.0 always appended. aws-nitro keeps its own NitroConfig, its
vsock delivery and its early return into krun_start_enter_nitro(), untouched.

Verified: cargo check -p libkrun --features blk,net is clean on
x86_64-pc-windows-msvc, x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu and aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu;
krun.dll links via winbuild.sh and still exports all nineteen symbols nerdbox
resolves; cargo fmt --all --check passes. On an aarch64 Linux host, against the
built libkrun.so, krun_set_exec now returns 0 where it returned -95, an unknown
context still returns -ENOENT, and krun_set_env/workdir/rlimits still return
-95. Instrumenting the assembly showed krun_env=" KRUN_INIT=/sbin/vminitd
\"TERM=xterm\" ..." and epilog=" -- \"-vsock-rpc-port=1025\" ..." with the call,
and the unchanged krun_env=" " with epilog=None without it. --features aws-nitro
still fails to build exactly as it did before this patch, on three pre-existing
errors that have nothing to do with exec (an unresolved aws_nitro::enclave
import from a feature that is not forwarded to the dependency, plus two net
gating errors).

Not verified: no Windows host, no WHP, and no guest were available here, so
nothing downstream of the host-side cmdline assembly has been run. That the
guest actually boots and vminitd actually connects back remains untested.
---
 include/libkrun.h      |   9 ++-
 src/libkrun/src/lib.rs | 177 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 2 files changed, 173 insertions(+), 13 deletions(-)

diff --git a/include/libkrun.h b/include/libkrun.h
index 6a84689..97af327 100644
--- a/include/libkrun.h
+++ b/include/libkrun.h
@@ -803,8 +803,12 @@ int32_t krun_set_workdir(uint32_t ctx_id,
  * to be passed to the executable, and the environment variables to be
  * configured in the context of the executable.
  *
- * Only available in aws-nitro builds. Non-nitro builds return -ENOTSUP;
- * use libkrun_init's Config::apply() with .krun_config.json instead.
+ * Available in every build. Upstream restricted this to aws-nitro builds in
+ * favour of libkrun_init's Config::apply() with .krun_config.json; this tree
+ * keeps the non-nitro implementation, which passes the workload to the guest
+ * on the kernel command line, because our consumers still drive libkrun
+ * through this call. Note that krun_set_env(), krun_set_workdir() and
+ * krun_set_rlimits() were *not* restored and still return -ENOTSUP.
  *
  * Arguments:
  *  "ctx_id"    - the configuration context ID.
@@ -816,7 +820,6 @@ int32_t krun_set_workdir(uint32_t ctx_id,
  *
  * Returns:
  *  Zero on success or a negative error number on failure.
- *  -ENOTSUP - not available in this build (non-nitro)
  */
 int32_t krun_set_exec(uint32_t ctx_id,
                       const char *exec_path,
diff --git a/src/libkrun/src/lib.rs b/src/libkrun/src/lib.rs
index 4a77bf5..c162222 100644
--- a/src/libkrun/src/lib.rs
+++ b/src/libkrun/src/lib.rs
@@ -186,12 +186,44 @@ struct NitroConfig {
     console_output: Option<PathBuf>,
 }
 
+/// Workload description supplied through `krun_set_exec()`.
+///
+/// Upstream commit 4d2201e ("libkrun: remove exec/env/workdir/rlimits support
+/// from non-nitro builds") moved these fields into `NitroConfig` and turned the
+/// non-nitro setters into `-ENOTSUP` stubs, on the grounds that callers should
+/// describe the workload with `Config::apply()` reading `/.krun_config.json`
+/// from the guest rootfs instead.
+///
+/// We restore the non-nitro path because nerdbox — the consumer this Windows
+/// port exists for — is pinned to libkrun v1.19.0's API: it calls
+/// `krun_set_exec()` exactly once, treats a non-zero return as fatal, and knows
+/// nothing about `.krun_config.json`. Adopting the replacement would mean
+/// patching nerdbox's guest image build as well as its host code, i.e. a larger
+/// divergence in a second component, to reach the same place. Re-pinning libkrun
+/// back to v1.19.0 is not an option either: the WHP backend postdates it.
+///
+/// Only `krun_set_exec()` is restored. `krun_set_env()`, `krun_set_workdir()`
+/// and `krun_set_rlimits()` stay `-ENOTSUP` stubs because nothing we build
+/// against calls them; restoring an API nobody exercises would be untested
+/// divergence for its own sake.
+#[cfg(not(feature = "aws-nitro"))]
+#[derive(Default)]
+struct ExecConfig {
+    exec_path: Option<String>,
+    /// Already collapsed into a kernel command-line fragment by
+    /// `collapse_str_array()`, i.e. each element individually double-quoted.
+    args: Option<String>,
+    env: Option<String>,
+}
+
 #[derive(Default)]
 struct ContextConfig {
     krunfw: KrunfwBindingsResult,
     vmr: VmResources,
     #[cfg(feature = "aws-nitro")]
     nitro: NitroConfig,
+    #[cfg(not(feature = "aws-nitro"))]
+    exec: ExecConfig,
     // Read when refusing a TSI port map after a NIC has been added, and when naming interfaces.
     #[cfg(any(unix, feature = "net"))]
     net_index: u8,
@@ -241,7 +273,43 @@ impl NitroConfig {
     }
 }
 
+#[cfg(not(feature = "aws-nitro"))]
+impl ExecConfig {
+    fn set_exec_path(&mut self, exec_path: String) {
+        self.exec_path = Some(exec_path);
+    }
+
+    fn set_env(&mut self, env: String) {
+        self.env = Some(env);
+    }
+
+    fn set_args(&mut self, args: String) {
+        self.args = Some(args);
+    }
+}
+
 impl ContextConfig {
+    /// The `KRUN_INIT=` cmdline token naming the guest workload, or `None` when
+    /// the caller never called `krun_set_exec()`. `None` is what keeps this
+    /// restoration invisible to callers on the `.krun_config.json` path.
+    #[cfg(not(feature = "aws-nitro"))]
+    fn get_exec_path(&self) -> Option<String> {
+        self.exec
+            .exec_path
+            .as_ref()
+            .map(|exec_path| format!("KRUN_INIT={exec_path}"))
+    }
+
+    #[cfg(not(feature = "aws-nitro"))]
+    fn get_env(&self) -> String {
+        self.exec.env.clone().unwrap_or_default()
+    }
+
+    #[cfg(not(feature = "aws-nitro"))]
+    fn get_args(&self) -> String {
+        self.exec.args.clone().unwrap_or_default()
+    }
+
     #[cfg(all(feature = "blk", not(feature = "tee")))]
     fn set_block_root(&mut self, device: String, fstype: Option<String>, options: Option<String>) {
         self.block_root = Some(BlockRootConfig {
@@ -1326,7 +1394,8 @@ pub unsafe extern "C" fn krun_set_workdir(ctx_id: u32, c_workdir_path: *const c_
     }
 }
 
-#[cfg(feature = "aws-nitro")]
+// Used by the aws-nitro setters and by the restored non-nitro krun_set_exec(),
+// so it is no longer gated on the feature.
 unsafe fn collapse_str_array(array: &[*const c_char]) -> Result<String, std::str::Utf8Error> {
     unsafe {
         let mut strvec = Vec::new();
@@ -1347,18 +1416,74 @@ unsafe fn collapse_str_array(array: &[*const c_char]) -> Result<String, std::str
 /// Set the executable to be run inside the VM, together with its arguments
 /// and environment variables.
 ///
-/// Only available in aws-nitro builds. Non-nitro builds return `-ENOTSUP`;
-/// use Config::apply() with .krun_config.json instead.
+/// Restored from libkrun v1.19.0. Upstream 4d2201e reduced this to an
+/// `-ENOTSUP` stub for non-nitro builds; see `ExecConfig` for why we diverge.
+/// The stored values are turned into kernel command-line tokens by
+/// `krun_start_enter()` — restoring this setter without that consumer would
+/// return success and then boot a guest with no workload configured, which is
+/// strictly worse than the stub's honest `-ENOTSUP`.
+#[allow(clippy::format_collect)]
 #[allow(clippy::missing_safety_doc)]
 #[unsafe(no_mangle)]
 #[cfg(not(feature = "aws-nitro"))]
 pub unsafe extern "C" fn krun_set_exec(
-    _ctx_id: u32,
-    _c_exec_path: *const c_char,
-    _c_argv: *const *const c_char,
-    _c_envp: *const *const c_char,
+    ctx_id: u32,
+    c_exec_path: *const c_char,
+    c_argv: *const *const c_char,
+    c_envp: *const *const c_char,
 ) -> i32 {
-    -libc::ENOTSUP
+    unsafe {
+        let exec_path = match CStr::from_ptr(c_exec_path).to_str() {
+            Ok(path) => path,
+            Err(e) => {
+                debug!("Error parsing exec_path: {e:?}");
+                return -libc::EINVAL;
+            }
+        };
+
+        let args = if !c_argv.is_null() {
+            let argv_array: &[*const c_char] = slice::from_raw_parts(c_argv, MAX_ARGS);
+            match collapse_str_array(argv_array) {
+                Ok(s) => s,
+                Err(e) => {
+                    debug!("Error parsing args: {e:?}");
+                    return -libc::EINVAL;
+                }
+            }
+        } else {
+            "".to_string()
+        };
+
+        let env = if !c_envp.is_null() {
+            let envp_array: &[*const c_char] = slice::from_raw_parts(c_envp, MAX_ARGS);
+            match collapse_str_array(envp_array) {
+                Ok(s) => s,
+                Err(e) => {
+                    debug!("Error parsing args: {e:?}");
+                    return -libc::EINVAL;
+                }
+            }
+        } else {
+            // NULL envp means "inherit the VMM's environment", as in v1.19.0.
+            // Spelled out here instead of via the `std::env` import, which is
+            // gated to Linux at the top of this file.
+            std::env::vars()
+                .map(|(key, value)| format!(" {key}=\"{value}\""))
+                .collect()
+        };
+
+        match CTX_MAP.lock().unwrap().entry(ctx_id) {
+            Entry::Occupied(mut ctx_cfg) => {
+                let cfg = ctx_cfg.get_mut();
+                cfg.exec.set_exec_path(exec_path.to_string());
+                cfg.exec.set_env(env);
+                cfg.exec.set_args(args);
+            }
+            Entry::Vacant(_) => return -libc::ENOENT,
+        }
+
+        KRUN_SUCCESS
+    }
 }
 
 /// Set the executable to be run inside the VM, together with its arguments
@@ -3395,10 +3520,42 @@ pub extern "C" fn krun_start_enter(ctx_id: u32) -> i32 {
         prolog.push(' ');
         prolog.push_str(arg);
     }
+    // Consumer for the workload restored in `krun_set_exec()`. The guest picks
+    // it up entirely through the kernel command line: the kernel hands init
+    // every unrecognised `KEY=value` token as an environment variable, and
+    // everything after `--` as init's argv[1..]. That reaches both kinds of
+    // guest init we care about — libkrun's own init, which reads `KRUN_INIT`
+    // and execs it (init/init-binary/src/main.rs), and a caller-supplied
+    // `init=` binary such as nerdbox's vminitd, which just wants its argv and
+    // environ. `krun_env` and `epilog` are still spliced into the cmdline by
+    // vmm's builder even when the caller supplied its own cmdline via
+    // `krun_set_kernel()`, which is the path nerdbox takes.
+    //
+    // Emitted only when `krun_set_exec()` was actually called, so callers using
+    // upstream's `.krun_config.json` mechanism get byte-identical cmdlines to
+    // before this patch.
+    #[cfg(not(feature = "aws-nitro"))]
+    let (krun_env, epilog) = match ctx_cfg.get_exec_path() {
+        Some(exec_path) => (
+            format!(
+                " {} {} {}",
+                exec_path,
+                ctx_cfg.get_block_root(),
+                ctx_cfg.get_env()
+            ),
+            Some(format!(" -- {}", ctx_cfg.get_args())),
+        ),
+        None => (format!(" {}", ctx_cfg.get_block_root()), None),
+    };
+    // aws-nitro returned into krun_start_enter_nitro() above; this is dead code
+    // there, and its workload is delivered over vsock rather than the cmdline.
+    #[cfg(feature = "aws-nitro")]
+    let (krun_env, epilog) = (format!(" {}", ctx_cfg.get_block_root()), None);
+
     let kernel_cmdline = KernelCmdlineConfig {
         prolog: Some(prolog),
-        krun_env: Some(format!(" {}", ctx_cfg.get_block_root())),
-        epilog: None,
+        krun_env: Some(krun_env),
+        epilog,
     };
 
     if ctx_cfg.vmr.set_kernel_cmdline(kernel_cmdline).is_err() {
